@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 2.78}Exercise 2.78:} The internal procedures in the
@code{scheme-number} package are essentially nothing more than calls to the
primitive arithmetic of the language.  It was not possible to use the
primitives directly because our type-tag system requires that each data
object have a type attached to it.  In fact, however, the runtime already has
a type system, which it uses internally: every datum is a variant of the
@code{Value} enum, and the tag functions can read that variant off.
Modify the definitions of @code{type_tag}, @code{contents}, and
@code{attach_tag} from @ref{2.4.2} so that our generic system takes
advantage of the runtime's internal type system.  That is to say, the
system should work as before except that ordinary numbers should be
represented simply as the runtime's own number variants rather than as
tagged data whose tag is the symbol @code{scheme-number}.
@end quotation

